The code statements that lead to the bug are:
return tracepoint_probe_register(call->tp,
					 call->class->perf_probe,
					 call);
Reason:
When `call->class->perf_probe` is NULL (as for synthetic events), this line passes a NULL function pointer to `tracepoint_probe_register()`, which dereferences it and triggers a kernel warning and potential crash.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
+		if (!call->class->perf_probe)
+			return -ENODEV;
		return tracepoint_probe_register(call->tp,
						 call->class->perf_probe,
						 call);
Reason:
The added guard returns -ENODEV before attempting to register with a NULL probe function. This prevents the NULL dereference and instead causes perf to report a clean "No such device" error, avoiding the kernel warning.

